# 6. Rebuild the Handwritten Notes Experience Properly

This is one of the most important improvements in the entire project.

I do not want ordinary paragraphs placed inside a box and described as handwritten notes. I want an attractive, realistic, well-organized study-notebook experience that combines the visual appeal of handwritten school notes with the accuracy and readability of professionally prepared learning material.

## 6.1 The appearance of the notes

Create a dedicated notes-reading experience with a paper-like visual design.

The page should resemble a carefully prepared student's notebook, with a subtle ruled or grid background, consistent margins, a clear heading hierarchy, and visually distinct sections.

Use a readable handwriting-style typeface for appropriate headings, annotations, short summaries, and highlighted terms. Use a highly readable font for longer explanations when a handwriting font would reduce clarity. The entire chapter must not become difficult to read simply to achieve a handwritten appearance.

Design each chapter as a sequence of well-composed notebook pages or sections. Each section should have enough whitespace to make it easy to revise, while avoiding unnecessarily large blank areas.

Use restrained colors to distinguish definitions, important concepts, formulas, examples, common mistakes, and examination tips. Use underlining, arrows, circles, margin annotations, and handwritten-style callouts sparingly so they emphasize important information rather than cluttering the page.

Headings, page labels, section dividers, and diagram styles should be consistent throughout all subjects.

The final design should feel like high-quality handwritten study notes that have been carefully reviewed and digitally organized.

## 6.2 Different subjects need different visual treatments

Do not use the same note template mechanically for every subject.

For Physics, emphasize correctly typeset equations, units, labelled diagrams, derivations, numerical examples, and step-by-step solutions.

For Chemistry, provide balanced chemical equations, reaction conditions, structures, periodic trends, appropriate tables, and worked examples where relevant.

For Biology, emphasize accurate labelled diagrams, biological processes, classification, comparisons, scientific terminology, and sequential flowcharts.

For Mathematics, use readable mathematical notation, properly aligned calculations, theorem statements, derivations, worked problems, and clearly explained steps.

For History and Geography, emphasize timelines, maps, cause-and-effect relationships, comparisons, important terminology, and concept diagrams.

For Computer Science, use code blocks, algorithms, flowcharts, examples, and step-by-step execution traces where appropriate.

These are examples of subject-specific presentation. Apply them according to the actual curriculum and content of each chapter.

## 6.3 Make diagrams genuinely useful

Every important diagram should help explain a concept.

Use accurate, legible diagrams with labels placed next to the relevant structures. Avoid distorted labels, illegible generated text, decorative illustrations that teach nothing, and diagrams that contradict the written explanation.

For equations, complex labels, scientific names, and detailed diagrams, prefer editable text, SVG, canvas-based rendering, or other structured rendering methods where appropriate.

If image generation is used for illustrative artwork, inspect the result and do not rely on generated text inside an image for essential educational information.

Provide captions and explanatory descriptions for important diagrams. Make them accessible and usable on mobile.

## 6.4 Structure the actual notes

Each chapter should contain the sections that genuinely apply to it:

- A chapter title and short introduction.
- Learning objectives.
- Topic-wise explanations in simple language.
- Important definitions and terminology.
- Key concepts and relationships.
- Formulas, units, laws, and derivations where relevant.
- Worked examples with complete reasoning.
- Diagrams, tables, timelines, or flowcharts as appropriate.
- Common misconceptions and mistakes.
- Important comparisons and revision points.
- Practice questions with answer explanations.
- A concise final revision sheet.
- A knowledge check that tests understanding.

Do not force irrelevant sections into a chapter. A history chapter should not contain meaningless formula headings, and a mathematics chapter should not contain an unrelated experiment section.

The notes should explain concepts, not merely list keywords. A student should be able to understand the chapter without needing to guess what each bullet point means.

## 6.5 Add useful notes-reader functionality

Implement these features using the existing application architecture:

- A chapter and topic table of contents.
- Navigation between topics and sections.
- Search within the current chapter.
- Bookmarks for important pages or topics.
- Personal annotations where feasible.
- Adjustable text size and reading preferences.
- Light and dark reading modes where they remain comfortable for studying.
- Progress indicators based on actual reading or completion events.
- Related quizzes and video lessons.
- Print-friendly layouts.
- PDF export when supported by the existing stack.
- Clear source information and content-review status.

Saved notes, bookmarks, and annotations must persist correctly for the appropriate user. Protect private annotations and learning records from unauthorized access.

PDF exports must preserve readable text, mathematical expressions, diagrams, headings, spacing, and sensible page breaks.

Do not add buttons for functionality that has not been implemented.

