# 11. Improve Video Lessons and Educational Animations

Video lessons must teach specific concepts clearly and accurately.

Each lesson should have a defined class, subject, chapter, topic, and learning objective. The script should introduce the question or concept, explain it in a logical sequence, show useful examples or visualizations, and finish with a concise recap.

Use educational animations, accurate labelled diagrams, meaningful transitions, and readable on-screen text. Avoid filling the screen with dense paragraphs, irrelevant stock footage, or visuals that do not match the narration.

For Biology, show accurate structures and processes. For Physics, visualize the relevant physical principles. For Chemistry, represent reactions and structures appropriately. For Mathematics, show calculations and diagrams step by step.

Generated visuals and scripts must be checked for incorrect scientific labels, misspellings, inconsistent terminology, and misleading demonstrations.

Where possible, provide captions, transcripts, playback controls, and lesson progress tracking.

If video generation depends on an external AI service, handle missing credentials, API failures, generation delays, and failed playback appropriately. Never display a successful-generation message until the operation has actually succeeded.

Reuse existing video infrastructure where possible instead of introducing unnecessary services or replacing a working integration.

