# 12. Improve Virtual Labs and 3D Learning

Interactive learning should be educationally meaningful, not simply visually impressive.

For every virtual lab, show the learning objective, relevant equipment or components, procedure, interactive controls, observations, explanation, and conclusion as appropriate to the experiment.

Make the simulation respond correctly to user actions. Check that calculations, displayed values, labels, and results are consistent with the underlying scientific principles.

Clearly distinguish simulated observations from real-world measurements.

For 3D learning, ensure that models load reliably, camera controls behave predictably, labels are readable, and the student can understand what each part represents. Connect each important model component to a useful explanation.

Optimize models and textures, avoid unnecessary rendering work, and provide a helpful fallback for unsupported devices or WebGL failures.

If an existing 3D anatomy viewer is present, improve its actual models, navigation, descriptions, and performance. Do not claim that a decorative model is anatomically complete or suitable for medical diagnosis.

